Rombie's changes to libretro-handy (Bro Code Technologies), for libretro-handy bc55d462, in the netplay engine. Handy's
own files are under its zlib license, whose second condition this header and the "Changed for Rombie" comments meet:
"Altered source versions must be plainly marked as such, and must not be misrepresented as being the original
software."

The boot ROM change, as handy/patches/handy.patch (2026-10-29): the core is built without lynx/lynxdec.cpp, the
decryption of a cart's loader that Handy's emulated boot ROM uses when the player gives no lynxboot.img. The file states
no license, so Rombie does not serve it.
- Makefile.common no longer compiles lynx/lynxdec.cpp; netplay/build.sh also deletes it from the build's copy.
- CSystem::HLE_BIOS_FE4A, the emulated boot ROM's only call into it, no longer decrypts or loads anything.
- retro_load_game refuses a cart (returns false, with an error in the log) when there is no valid lynxboot.img,
  missing or failing Handy's CRC check. A BS93 homebrew file loads straight into RAM and still needs none.

The ComLynx link (2026-11-18): up to eight Lynxes in the one core, their serial ports on one cable, so every PC in a
netplay session runs every console and only the pads travel. With one console (the default) nothing below applies and
the core plays as it did.
- Core options: handy_link_consoles (1-8, read as the game loads), handy_link_screen and handy_link_audio (the
  console, 1 to handy_link_consoles, this PC shows and hears), handy_link_slice (the system cycles each console runs
  in its turn, 1024 by default) and handy_link_stagger (the frames between one console's power-on and the next's, 20
  by default, up to 600, read as the game loads).
- Handy keeps a Lynx's timing and CPU flags in globals (lynx/system.h). Each linked console keeps its own copy
  (HandyGlobals), put in place before it runs (HandyGlobalsLoad) and taken back after (HandyGlobalsSave), and its
  sound buffer, gAudioBuffer, is now a pointer to the console's own. The function statics that carry from one call to
  the next are members of the console's own objects: CCart::CartAddressStrobe's last strobe, CMikie::UpdateSound's
  last samples, and CSusie::PaintSprites' quad offsets.
- retro_run: console n reads input port n. The consoles take turns, a slice of system cycles each, until each has run
  the frame's cycles. Only the shown console's picture goes out and only the heard one's sound, though every console's
  sound moves on alike, since a state holds where it is. Select doesn't turn the screen with more than one console, and
  handy_rot can't change after the start: a state doesn't hold the rotation, which follows the cart.
- Power-on: console n is switched on n * (handy_link_stagger frames and 1/handy_link_consoles of a frame) after
  console 1, as players switch theirs on a moment apart, and hears nothing on the cable before. Started on the same
  cycle, the consoles run alike to the cycle and make the same choices from what they hear, and the games' link never
  forms (Gauntlet: The Third Encounter, checked). The part of a frame spreads their frames, so their vertical blanks,
  where Epyx's RedEye protocol sends each player's state in play, don't fall together as they would on one clock.
- The cable: with more than one console, every console has a cable present (ComLynxCable), and the wire replaces
  Handy's receive queue and loopback. ComLynx is one line every console drives and hears, idle high, a low bit from
  any console winning. A console's SERDAT write (and a break) puts a frame of nine bits on the wire, the ninth the
  parity bit Mikey sends (or PAREVEN with parity off), until its transmit countdown ends (CMikie::ComLynxLinkCallback,
  the wire in libretro.cpp, timed on the frame clock the consoles share). After each round of turns, every frame that
  has ended goes into the one receive register of every console that is on (CMikie::ComLynxLinkRx), the sender's too
  (its echo), merged with each frame that overlapped it: two consoles sending at once both get the AND of their
  frames, and a framing error when they did not start together, so each sees its echo differ from what it sent, as
  RedEye checks. A byte still unread is overrun, and with parity on, a wrong ninth bit sets the parity error that
  Handy's SERCTL lacked (bit 4), all until RESETERR.
- Timers: a one-shot timer that has stopped, done, counts from the moment a CTLB write starts it again, as Mikey's
  does. Handy counted the time it had stood still at once and fired at its next update, which cut RedEye's receive
  timeout (timer 1, started again at each byte) short after the first byte of every message, so no message ever came
  through whole.
- The state: "HLNK", the number of consoles, the frames since power-on and the wire's frames, then for each console its
  size, its ContextSave and what ContextSave leaves out that a linked console needs back exactly
  (CSystem::LinkContextSave): Mikie's receive queue (the bytes, both pointers, the count waiting, the framing, overrun
  and parity errors), its display DMA (mLynxLine, mLynxLineDMACounter, mLynxAddr and the display pointer's offset), its
  pan and attenuation and UpdateSound's last samples; Susie's quad offsets and its math unit's signs; and the cart's
  last strobe. A state for another number of consoles is refused. With one console the state is the solo core's,
  ContextSave alone, so a solo state loads and loads back in solo play.
- Memory: console n's EEPROM is (n << 8) | RETRO_MEMORY_SAVE_RAM, n = 1 to handy_link_consoles, read and written in
  place (CEEPROM::Data), so the frontend reads and writes it without a reset. System RAM is console 1's on every PC.
  Console n past 1 saves its EEPROM file with ".n" after the name.

